            <ArticleTitle>An uncommon cause of anterior knee pain: patellar chondroma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>81-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MPH.0b013e318292bc9d</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 16-year-old boy presented with a patellar mass and anterior knee pain without any trauma. On physical and radiologic assessment, a mass at the superolateral edge of the patella and a hyperintense lesion on T2 sequences of magnetic resonance imaging was detected. Excisional biopsy revealed a chondroma of patella. Primary bone tumors of the patella are extremely rare and occurence of chondroma in this localization is very uncommon. Although anterior knee pain is a very frequent and usually harmless, it is essential to consider the more severe disorders such as bone tumors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
